      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'd be happy to provide you with a timeline of the main events of the Mau Mau uprising from the perspective of the Kikuyu fighters and marginalized groups resisting British colonial rule. Here's a key timeframe: \n\n- 1930s: Kikuyu discontent grows as British impose collective land ownership and restrict local economy, hurting Kikuyu farmers.\n- 1940s: Bareku groups (Kikuyu bands with Special Reserve units) in the British Army begin questioning orders from colonial authorities and report returning to the land prompting local membership in the Mau Mau.\n- 1952: The British declare an emergency, executing British Kenya African Union (BKAU) moderate leaders and pushing Kikuyu UMCG chairman Jomo Kenyatta's membership in the struggle public.\n- January 1953: Kenyatta arrested and denied local laws apply to diplomacy deadlock, while grassroots fights grow.\n- June 1956: The British re-summon the Indian Governor Lain Singh Chitet to visit Kenya.\n- 1957-1960: Open dissidents/ Activists resume burning farms in profitable midlands Samburu Tana Pembe openness occurring.\n\n\n\nWould you like me to add more details or amend",
